    {
      "title": "Effect of Interest‑Rate Increase on Exchange Rate and Aggregate Demand",
      "source_segment_ids": ["CON_0043", "CON_0044", "CON_0049", "CON_0050", "CON_0051", "CON_0052"],
      "mode": "visual_only",
      "remaining_text": null,
      "visuals": [
        {
          "source_segment_ids": ["CON_0043", "CON_0044", "CON_0049", "CON_0050", "CON_0051", "CON_0052"],
          "needs_review": false,
          "missing_information": [],
          "evidence": [
            {
              "note_id": "CON_0043",
              "exact_quote": "An increase in the interest rate reduces aggregate demand because investment falls and the exchange rate appreciates, lowering net exports."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "CON_0044",
              "exact_quote": "In an open economy, monetary policy affects aggregate demand through two channels: investment reduction and exchange‑rate appreciation."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "CON_0049",
              "exact_quote": "When I increase interest rate from here to there, then my exchange rate has to appreciate."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "CON_0050",
              "exact_quote": "So an expansionary domestic monetary policy will lead to a contraction in output, which is what we get out of monetary policy, but it will also lead to an appreciation of the currency."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "CON_0051",
              "exact_quote": "And the compensation will come through an expected capital loss through the currency."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "CON_0052",
              "exact_quote": "And of course, the precision is already built in here, which is what makes monetary policy more powerful than the closed economy, because you get the next export channel."
            }
          ],
